    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-4986",
      "problem": "The diagram below shows the function $y=f(x).$ Plot the graph of $y=2f(x)+2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14215498/assets/243f8af2f2fe8e826866c228c5662baca03da66407e3cc7ba435ce80d3a9a7fe.png)",
      "worked_solution": "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=2f(x)+2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=2f(x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14215498/assets/cef2e07bd22dab7d69f51a0197960bcdd980d5412ee47237b387dad90a4655f9.png)\n\n- Then, translate it up by $2$ units to get the graph of $y=2f(x) + 2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14215498/assets/a55f6aecd289cc144e621d8ed7e298d51af50523d06b7455cbec9cb3fb58dd55.png)",
